::: {.doc-subject}

Check if graph is bipartite (2-colorable).
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L19006-L19106){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.is_bipartite()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Is bipartite](../assets/graphs/is-bipartite.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Left: a two-by-three ladder whose nodes split into two colors, A, C, E and B, D, F, with every edge joining different colors, so is_bipartite() returns True. Right: the same ladder plus a diagonal between A and E, which closes the odd triangle A, B, E (shown in the accent color), so is_bipartite() returns False." width="100%"}

A graph is bipartite if its nodes can be divided into two independent sets such that every
edge connects nodes from different sets. Equivalently, the graph can be colored with two
colors such that no adjacent nodes share a color.

All trees and forests are bipartite. Graphs with odd-length cycles (including self-loops)
are not. On a directed graph edge direction is ignored, as in `bipartite_sets()`.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   `True` if the graph is bipartite, `False` otherwise.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~bipartite_sets`) 

:   The two sides of a bipartite graph.

[](`~is_bipartite_node_set`) 

:   Check whether a given set of nodes is one side.

[](`~layout_bipartite`) 

:   Draw the two sides in two rows or columns.

[](`~greedy_color`) 

:   Color the nodes with as few colors as it can, two or more.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A path is bipartite, since its nodes can alternate between two sides along the chain. A
triangle isn't: whichever sides A and B take, C is joined to both, so it can't join either:

::: {#21693235 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

path = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("B", "C")])
triangle = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "A")])

path.is_bipartite(), triangle.is_bipartite()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


### Workers and tasks

The teams network joins each worker on a software team to the tasks they can do. Every edge
runs from a worker to a task, so it's bipartite:

::: {#4fee7287 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
team = ec.teams()

team.is_bipartite()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
True
```
:::
:::


`bipartite_sets()` recovers the two sides, the workers and the tasks, from the edges alone:

::: {#9d2e1fd5 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
workers, tasks = team.bipartite_sets()

[team.get_label_from_id(n) for n in tasks]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
['Backend',
 'Frontend',
 'Database',
 'Design',
 'Testing',
 'Docs',
 'DevOps',
 'Security']
```
:::
:::


### One edge breaks it

An edge between two workers closes an odd cycle if they can do a task in common. Priya and
Tom can both do the backend, so when Priya starts mentoring Tom the graph is no longer
bipartite. `is_bipartite()` records its answer in the step log, so `play()` shows the odd
cycle it found on its last step, with the workers and tasks drawn in two rows:

::: {#9b4985c1 .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
team.layout_bipartite(align="horizontal", aspect_ratio=0.4)

team.note("Priya starts mentoring Tom.").add_edge("Priya", "Tom")
team.is_bipartite()

team.play(layout="pos", height=320)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}
